const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Systematic Review Writing Service","serviceType":"Systematic review, evidence synthesis and meta-analysis support","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/systematic-review/","description":"Protocol and PROSPERO registration, reproducible searches, dual screening, risk of bias and PRISMA 2020 reporting.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"},"hasOfferCatalog":{"@type":"OfferCatalog","name":"Systematic review packages","itemListElement":[{"@type":"Offer","name":"Evidence summary — structured summary of studies you already have","description":"A structured evidence summary rather than a full systematic review: protocol registration, dual screening and risk-of-bias assessment are not included and are added at Complete. For early-career researchers and postgraduates working from studies already collected."},{"@type":"Offer","name":"Focused — a narrower question, up to three databases","description":"Protocol and PROSPERO registration, a reproducible search across up to three databases, dual independent screening, extraction, risk of bias and a PRISMA 2020 manuscript. Turnaround 10–15 business days. From USD 580.","priceSpecification":{"@type":"PriceSpecification","price":"580","priceCurrency":"USD"}},{"@type":"Offer","name":"Complete — a full systematic review","description":"Multi-database search across up to four databases plus grey literature, dual screening, duplicate extraction, risk of bias, meta-analysis or narrative synthesis and GRADE per outcome. Turnaround 30–40 business days. From USD 850.","priceSpecification":{"@type":"PriceSpecification","price":"850","priceCurrency":"USD"}},{"@type":"Offer","name":"Advanced — review, meta-analysis and submission support","description":"The full review with meta-analysis and GRADE, plus journal selection, formatting, submission and response to reviewer comments through to a decision. Turnaround 60–120 business days. From USD 1,450.","priceSpecification":{"@type":"PriceSpecification","price":"1450","priceCurrency":"USD"}},{"@type":"Offer","name":"Rewriting and enhancement — a review that was rejected","description":"A written methodological critique first, then structural and methodological repairs, rewriting against the reviewer and editor comments received, language editing, reference reformatting and a plagiarism and AI-content recheck. From USD 690 per manuscript.","priceSpecification":{"@type":"PriceSpecification","price":"690","priceCurrency":"USD"}}]}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What are the steps in conducting a systematic review?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Nine, and they run in one order for a reason.\n\nFrame an answerable question in PICO, PIRD, CoCoPop or SPIDER.\nWrite a protocol that fixes the eligibility criteria, outcomes and analysis in advance.\nRegister it, usually on PROSPERO.\nBuild and run a search strategy across the databases the question needs.\nDeduplicate and screen every record on title and abstract, with two reviewers working independently.\nRetrieve and assess the full texts, recording a reason for each exclusion.\nExtract data into a piloted form, in duplicate.\nAssess risk of bias study by study.\nSynthesise, grade the certainty of the evidence and report the whole thing under PRISMA 2020.\n\nNothing in that list can be done retrospectively without weakening it — which is why a review written up after the reading is done does not meet the definition of a systematic review, however thorough the reading was — and we will tell you at the scoping stage which of the two you have."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between a systematic review and a meta-analysis?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A systematic review is the method: a pre-specified question, an exhaustive search, transparent selection criteria, critical appraisal and a synthesis of what the studies found. A meta-analysis is one possible synthesis within it — the statistical pooling of results into a single effect estimate with a confidence interval. Every meta-analysis should sit inside a systematic review; not every systematic review should contain a meta-analysis.\n\nWhere studies differ too much in population, intervention or outcome measure, pooling them produces a precise-looking number that means nothing, and the right answer is a structured narrative synthesis reported under SWiM. Deciding which of the two you are doing is a protocol-stage decision, not a results-stage one."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How does a systematic review make findings more reliable?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"By removing the places where an author's judgement can quietly decide the answer. Pre-specifying the criteria stops studies being included because they agree with you. Searching several databases rather than one stops the answer being decided by what PubMed happens to index. Dual independent screening stops a single reader's fatigue becoming an exclusion. Appraising risk of bias stops a badly conducted trial carrying the same weight as a well conducted one. Registering the protocol stops the outcome being chosen after the results are visible.\n\nNone of these is about working harder; each one closes a specific route by which a review reaches a conclusion its evidence does not support."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which databases and tools should I use?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The question decides. A clinical intervention review is expected to search MEDLINE, Embase and the Cochrane Library at minimum, and reviewers will ask about all three. Nursing and allied health questions need CINAHL; psychology and behavioural questions need APA PsycINFO; agriculture and veterinary questions need CAB Abstracts; anything with trials needs ClinicalTrials.gov and the WHO ICTRP for unpublished and terminated studies. Web of Science and Scopus add citation searching, which finds what subject indexing misses.\n\nOn the tooling side, references are managed in EndNote, Zotero or Mendeley, screening is done in Covidence or Rayyan so that two reviewers can work blind to each other, and analysis in R, Stata or RevMan. The tools matter far less than the strategy: a good strategy in one platform beats a poor one in six."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How are data collected, extracted and analysed?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Extraction runs from a form piloted on three or four studies before it is used on the rest, because the fields you wish you had captured always become obvious at study twelve. Two reviewers extract every study independently and reconcile, which is where most transcription errors are caught. Where a paper reports an outcome only in a figure, or reports a median where you need a mean, the corresponding author is contacted and the provenance of the number recorded rather than quietly imputed.\n\nAnalysis follows the pre-specified plan: pooled effect estimates with the model stated, heterogeneity quantified, pre-planned subgroup and sensitivity analyses, and publication-bias assessment where there are enough studies to make a funnel plot meaningful."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I have to register the protocol on PROSPERO?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"There is no legal obligation to, and journals increasingly expect it. A growing number of journals decline unregistered reviews outright, AMSTAR 2 treats the absence of a registered protocol as a critical weakness, and without a registration you have no way of demonstrating that the outcomes you report are the ones you set out to measure.\n\nRegistration is free, takes a few hours once the protocol is written, and is accepted for reviews that have not yet begun screening. Where PROSPERO does not accept a review type — scoping reviews, for instance — the equivalent is registering on the Open Science Framework or publishing the protocol. Deviations after registration are not a problem in themselves; undeclared deviations are."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How long does a systematic review take?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"An academic team working around other commitments typically takes twelve to eighteen months. With Pubrica running the work, a complete review is usually thirty to forty business days, and sixty to one hundred and twenty where a meta-analysis, GRADE and submission support are included.\n\nWhat drives the time is the volume of evidence rather than the difficulty of the topic: screening nine thousand records is six times the work of screening fifteen hundred, whatever the question. A rapid review can be delivered far faster using systematic methods with declared shortcuts — fewer databases, single screening with verification, a date limit — and the shortcuts are then reported as limitations rather than hidden."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What usually goes wrong, and what do reviewers reject reviews for?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Six things, in roughly this order of frequency. A search that covered one database, or that cannot be reproduced from what is written in the methods. A PRISMA flow diagram whose numbers do not reconcile. No full-text exclusion list, or one without reasons. Studies pooled that should not have been, usually visible as heterogeneity above seventy-five per cent with no explanation offered. No risk-of-bias assessment, or one performed but never used to weigh the conclusion. And a question that turns out to have been answered by a review published while yours was being written.\n\nFive of those six are set before any writing begins, which is why the protocol and the search are where the money is best spent."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you help with a review I have already started, or one that was rejected?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and most of our work arrives that way. Come with a protocol and we run the search; come with a screened set and we extract, appraise and synthesise; come with a full draft and we rewrite it.\n\nFor a rejected manuscript the first deliverable is a written critique rather than a revision, because it has to say honestly whether the problem is repairable. Structure, reporting, appraisal and synthesis can all be fixed after the fact. A search that missed a required database cannot — it has to be re-run and re-screened, and a rewrite that pretends otherwise will fail at the same point with the next journal. We will tell you which of the two you have before you commit to anything."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who is named as an author, and how is AI use declared?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"You are. Authorship under ICMJE requires substantial contribution to conception or design, or to acquisition, analysis or interpretation; drafting or critically revising; approving the final version; and agreeing to be accountable for it. Pubrica supports all four but takes none of them in your place, and we do not accept authorship on client work. Where our contribution should appear in the acknowledgements — and on a review where we built the search and ran the synthesis, it should — we say so and draft the wording.\n\nOn artificial intelligence: any AI-assisted screening or drafting is declared in the methods, with what was used and for which step, because ICMJE and COPE both now require it and an undeclared tool found later is treated as a research integrity issue rather than a style preference."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How is a systematic review priced, and what drives the cost?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"By how much evidence there is to handle, not by word count. Four things move the figure: how many databases the question needs, how many records come back after de-duplication, whether the included studies can be pooled or only described, and whether you are starting from a topic, a protocol or a draft that came back from review.\n\nThe first of those is the one worth attention, because a search that returns four thousand records to find forty studies spends most of the budget discarding. You can see the shape of it before you speak to anyone: the free screening estimator turns your record count into reviewer hours, elapsed weeks and a cost, stage by stage, with every rate an editable field."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can a systematic review be published without a meta-analysis?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and a great many should be. Pooling requires the studies to be answering a similar enough question with similar enough methods that one number describes them all, and quite often they are not. A review that reports a structured narrative synthesis under SWiM — with the effect direction, magnitude and certainty set out study by study, and an explicit statement of why pooling was not appropriate — is a stronger paper than one that produces a pooled estimate nobody believes.\n\nThe decision belongs in the protocol, with the criterion stated in advance. Deciding after seeing the forest plot is the version reviewers object to, so we settle it in the protocol."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How many people does a systematic review team actually need?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Five roles, though not five people. Two independent screeners, because dual screening roughly halves the number of eligible studies wrongly excluded and is what Cochrane requires. An information specialist for the search, which is the single highest-leverage hour in the whole project. A content expert who can tell a clinically irrelevant comparison from a relevant one. A statistician if anything is going to be pooled. And someone who owns the protocol and the PROSPERO record. On our projects those roles are named in the handover, so you can see who did which stage."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can we use AI to screen records, and will a journal accept it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Automation-assisted prioritisation — ranking records so the likely includes surface first — is well established and cuts title and abstract time substantially. Using a large language model to make include or exclude decisions unsupervised is a different thing and is not yet defensible at the screening stage of a review that will be published.\n\nWhat matters either way is disclosure: journals and PRISMA both now expect you to state which tool was used, at which stage, with what human oversight, and ICMJE added a section on artificial intelligence in publishing in its January 2026 update. We use prioritisation, we say so in the methods, and two humans still read the included set."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do we handle disagreement between screeners?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Disagreement is the point of screening twice, so it is recorded rather than quietly resolved. Conflicts at title and abstract go to a third reviewer or to discussion, with the rule agreed in the protocol before screening starts.\n\nAt full text, every exclusion is recorded with its reason, because those reasons appear in the PRISMA flow diagram and an editor will check that they add up. We report the agreement statistic where the journal expects one, while noting that a high kappa on an easy set of records means less than it appears; what a reviewer actually wants to see is that the disagreements existed and were adjudicated on a stated criterion."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Research services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Systematic review","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/systematic-review/"}]}];

